# UI render work must stay proportional to its data

Applies to `ui/src/**` code that iterates over a large or unbounded collection
during render, in a `useMemo`, or in a selector. The shipped catalogs are the
main case: `AGGREGATOR_APP_CATALOG` (about 1,600 entries), the composio and
arcade catalogs, and `APP_DEFINITIONS` / `CONNECTABLE_APP_DEFINITIONS`. Lists
loaded from the API that grow with a company's data, such as tasks, runs,
agents, connections and activity, count too.

## Why

On Paperclip Cloud, `/apps` pegged the CPU after a helper that scanned the whole
aggregator catalog was called once per native row, for every catalog app. That
is about 1,600 × 110 × 1,600 name comparisons, roughly 200 s of main-thread work
per render. The memo also re-ran on every render because one of its
dependencies was a `Map` rebuilt in the component body. The tests passed
because they replaced the catalog with one or two entries, and the slow branch
only ran on Cloud.

## Requirements

- Do not call a function that scans a collection from inside a loop over
  another large collection. Build a `Set` or `Map` index once, outside the loop,
  and look items up in it.
- Normalize keys (identity, slug, lowercase name) once per item, not inside
  comparison callbacks.
- Every `useMemo` dependency must keep its identity between renders that change
  no data. Do not pass objects, arrays or `Map`s rebuilt in the component body.
  Memoize them, or derive them from query `data`, which TanStack Query keeps
  structurally shared. `useQueries` returns a new array on every render.
- When a change adds or reorders work over a shipped catalog, at least one test
  must run that path with the real catalog. Load it with `vi.importActual`, and
  use Cloud mode when the code branches on `useCloudInstance()`. Bound the work
  with a deterministic counter, not wall-clock time. A counter that throws
  above its limit makes a runaway render fail at once instead of stalling the
  test worker. See the "full Cloud catalog" test in
  `ui/src/pages/apps/Browse.test.tsx`.

## Exceptions

- Small fixed lists (status options, tabs, a handful of providers) need none of
  this.
- A nested scan is fine when both sides are bounded and small, for example one
  task's comments matched against its attachments.

## Review criteria

- Flag nested iteration where both collections can be large, especially when
  the inner call hides a scan (`some`, `find`, `filter`, `includes` on an array).
- Flag `useMemo` or `useCallback` dependencies that are rebuilt every render.
- Flag branches gated on Cloud, experimental settings or feature flags whose
  only tests use toy-sized fixtures when the branch iterates a shipped catalog.
